      "thinking": "I'm digging into the metrics discrepancy \u2014 nobody noticed anything user-facing, but the shop's metrics simply stopped arriving. Comparing error rates before and after, ClientDisconnected looks similar, but the typical rate for Gbac1 climbed noticeably, which seems like a clue worth chasing.\n\nI'll go with true, since the overlapping ranges and small sample sizes make the apparent rise unreliable to treat as a real anomaly.\n\nNow I'm working through the causal chain of symptoms: nobody using the service noticed anything wrong, and separately the shop's metrics stopped arriving after 01:04, citing the relevant log entries for each claim without conflating them.\n\nNext I'm tracing why the scrape failed \u2014 the ServiceMonitor selects a port named `metrics`, but the deployed revision renamed it to `http-metrics`, so nothing matched and the target list came up empty, as stated in the source comment rather than directly observed. Then I move on to why the port name changed in that revision in the first place.\n\nSince the alert is MetricsAbsent and samples were still arriving at 01:04, after P1's 21:05 deploy, that confirms metrics were healthy with P1 in place, so P1 can be ruled out citing T5 and D1. Checking flag changes in T16 shows none in the hour before onset.\n\nThe real chain: no safeguard checks that the scrape config's port name matches the service's actual port name (per S1), so when P2 renamed the port, the scrape stopped matching anything \u2014 that's the missing-safeguard contributing factor plus the actual break. This reaches a genuine cause, resting on reading S1/S2, not just the flag data: the root cause is the ServiceMonitor in deploy/scrape.yaml selecting the shop's metrics port by a name that no longer exists after the rename.\n\nPhrasing the cause plainly: a configuration change renamed the connection used to collect the shop's measurements, but the monitoring setup still looked for the old name and found nothing. No numbers needed here. The mismatch is still unresolved and metrics remain uncollected.\n\nAs for lasting risk, the real exposure is that any genuine fault in the shop would go unseen by metric-based alerts, and since nothing validates that the two port names agree, a future rename on either side could silence the metrics again just as easily. For contributing factors, I note there's no check confirming the scrape port name matches the metrics port name\u2014the comment in the file confirms nothing verifies this.\n\nOn fixes, the PR adding a test addresses catching this before deploy, though that test hasn't actually run yet since the PR is still pending review and merge. I'm also considering whether a detection-side improvement makes sense\u2014something like alerting directly when a ServiceMonitor selects zero targets, so an empty selection surfaces immediately rather than manifesting only as missing metrics.\n\nThe bigger process gap is that the review for D1 didn't flag the scrape config's dependency on the port name, and since the rename was applied estate-wide, other services may have the same unnoticed mismatch. That makes a good preventive action item: audit other services that underwent the same port rename to confirm their scrape configs aren't still pointing at old port names.\n\nSeparately, MetricsAbsent fired but paged nobody, which meant no one was prompted to act even though the fix was simple -- worth adding a detect-oriented action item to route that alert to on-call paging.\n\nAI-4 (detect): add an alert for a ServiceMonitor whose target list ends up empty, catching selector mismatches directly.\n\nAI-5 (mitigate): since merging the fix alone doesn't help until it's deployed, add a separate action item to deploy the revision and confirm samples resume.\n\nNow ordering the factors: no check currently verifies scrape port names match metrics.portName, which is the core gap the PR review addresses; the estate-wide rename also wasn't traced through its dependents, which is the risk the audit (AI-3) and empty-target alert (AI-5) both cover from different angles.\n\nI'll drop the empty-target-alert factor since it's too tangential, and keep the paging factor: nobody was paged, so no one picked up the draft fix sooner, which I'll attribute to AI-4. Now moving into the lessons section, starting with what went well\u2014Argus connecting the gap to P2's work.\n\nFor what went wrong, I'm noting the Investigator's claim that service still served correctly doesn't hold up as a real factor, while the missing page is a genuine one. I'm also considering whether F1 being written against main rather than against c3c36da is actually a problem, since S1 shows the same mismatch there anyway, so it's fine. There's also a question of whether a forward-fix to the scrape config (rather than a rollback) could have restored metrics during the response, since Argus only weighed rollback as an option.\n\nD1 also renamed the web and admin ports in the same change, and nothing in the record shows what, if anything, selects those by name \u2014 that's a latent risk rather than a confirmed outcome, so I'll leave got_lucky empty. For assumptions, I should note the empty target list wasn't directly read from Prometheus but inferred from S1's comment and the name mismatch, and that F1's fix was never actually run.\n\nI need to figure out how to cite specific figures carefully \u2014 01:04 maps to {T5.2}, 3 replicas maps to {T13.2}, and I should avoid citing port 9090 unless {D1.4} is explicitly referenced. For the chain's third step, I'm framing it as: revision c3c36da deployed at {P2.1}, which renamed the ports, with all {T13.2} replicas running it as of {T13.1}. P1 gets ruled out here because samples kept arriving up until {T5.2} while that revision was in place.\n\n",
